What to implement
- Cache-first, then provider
  - Two-tier cache: in-process LRU (very hot items) + Redis (cross-instance). TTL 1–24h based on traffic. Normalize keys: sha256(text) + provider + source + target + options.
  - Configure Redis not to grow indefinitely: set maxmemory and use an eviction policy like allkeys-lfu or volatile-lru.
- In-flight de-duplication
  - Prevent concurrent identical requests from all hitting the provider by sharing the same promise (singleflight). Optional: use a short-lived Redis lock for multi-instance.
- Micro-batching (optional but effective)
  - DeepL accepts multiple text params in one call. Aggregate requests per (source,target,provider) for 10–20ms before flushing to the provider. This reduces overhead without hurting latency.
- Connection reuse and tight timeouts
  - Use keep-alive HTTP agent and short timeouts (connect 100ms, overall 800–1200ms). Abort on client cancel. Prefer HTTP/2 if your client supports it.
- Resilient retries
  - Exponential backoff with jitter for 429/5xx; honor Retry-After; cap total time so p95 stays predictable. Optional fallback to a secondary provider if primary continues to fail.
- Stale-while-revalidate
  - If there’s a cached translation that’s “stale,” return it immediately and refresh in the background to keep sub-300ms UX.
- Respect user prefs
